43, "fundamentals": {"margin": 4.72, "lean": -4.0, "environment": 8.7, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.02}, "rating_margin": -1.0, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 6.23, "baseline_margin": 0.81, "analyst_adjustment": 0.69, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price Garcia clearly ahead (66-69% Dem)", "Recent polls split: Emerson +2 Dem, WaPo/Schar -1 Rep", "Ratings divided between Tossup and Lean R flip, with Split Ticket at Lean D", "Both newcomers well funded with heavy outside spending on a redrawn district"], "rationale": "The polling average is essentially even and most raters call this a Tossup, but prediction markets and Split Ticket lean toward Garcia, and the Democratic-leaning Normington Petts polling plus Jeffries campaigning suggest modest Democratic momentum. A small Democratic edge of about a point and a half fits the evidence without straying far from the even baseline.", "watch": "Any late polling movement or a shift in how the redistricting backlash plays out in South Texas could tip this either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": []}, "overview": "Texas's 35th District is a newly redrawn San Antonio-area seat that Republicans drew to their advantage, yet it has emerged as one of the closest House races of 2024's cycle. Republican Carlos De La Cruz and Democrat Johnny Garcia, both political newcomers who do not live in the district, are running well-funded campaigns with heavy outside spending on each side. Neither candidate has incumbency advantages, and national figures from both parties are paying attention because the seat could help decide House control.\n\nRecent polls are nearly tied: a Washington Post/Schar survey put De La Cruz up one, while Emerson and Democratic-sponsored polling gave Garcia small leads. Expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean Republican, though prediction markets clearly favor Garcia. The forecast lands on a razor-thin Democratic edge with wide uncertainty, appropriate for a tossup seat with limited polling.", "hi